explanation:
## Both directions are one pass with an inner loop

The shape that makes this easy is the same in both: an outer index that jumps by a whole unit, and an inner loop that works out how big that unit is. The mistake that makes it hard is advancing by one and patching up afterwards.

### Encoding

```cpp
while (i < s.size())
{
    std::size_t j{ i };
    while (j < s.size() && s[j] == s[i]) ++j;   // how far does this run go

    out += s[i];
    if (j - i > 1) out += std::to_string(j - i);

    i = j;                                       // past the RUN, not past one
}
```

`i = j` is the line that matters. Writing `++i` there and keeping a separate counter works too, but it needs a flush after the loop ends, and a forgotten flush is the commonest way to lose the last run.

**`std::to_string`, not `'0' + n`.** `out += char('0' + run)` produces the right character for a run of nine and garbage for a run of ten — `'0' + 12` is `'<'`. The hidden cases include a run of twelve and a run of ninety-nine for exactly this reason. It is the same class of mistake as assuming a single digit anywhere else.

### Decoding

```cpp
const char c{ s[i] };
++i;

std::size_t n{ 0 };
while (i < s.size() && s[i] >= '0' && s[i] <= '9')   // ALL the digits
{
    n = n * 10 + (s[i] - '0');
    ++i;
}

out.append(n == 0 ? 1 : n, c);
```

The inner `while` is the mirror image of the one in `encode`, and omitting it is the mirror-image bug: reading one digit turns `a12` into one `a` followed by a literal `2`, instead of twelve `a`s.

`n == 0 ? 1 : n` is how "no digits at all" is spelled. A bare character means one of it, and zero is not a count that encoding can ever produce.

### Why it is a round trip

The test cases come in pairs on purpose. An encoder and a decoder that agree with each other but not with the specification will pass neither half, and an encoder that is right while the decoder is not will pass exactly half — which tells you immediately which direction to look in.

## The reading that makes it fail

A plausible encoding of `abbaabb` is `a1b2a2b2`, writing the count every time. That is a perfectly good scheme; it is not this one, and it is longer for text with few repeats, which is the case run-length encoding is usually applied to. The specification says a run of one is written bare, so `ab2a2b2` is the answer and `a1b2a2b2` is wrong. Reading the examples closely is part of the exercise.

## Where this actually appears

In PCX and BMP bitmaps, in fax Group 3 encoding, and inside almost every general-purpose compressor as the cheap first stage before something cleverer. The real versions escape their count byte so the payload may contain digits — which is the restriction lifted here by promising lowercase letters only.
